  "worked_solution": "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ approaches $0$ from the left and right we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=3,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=3\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.$\n\n- Statement III is true.  We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)=2\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=2.$ Meanwhile, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=2\n$$\n\n \nHence ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=2.$ Therefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x).$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true.",
